  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist in Kibana, and the rule is enabled and running without errors.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-1e537256 Web logs overview`** (id `fa5ef396-58a8-422b-8f37-c738dcc76e02`)\n- Markdown header.\n- **Total requests**: count over `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- **Total bytes transferred**: sum of `bytes`.\n- **Server error (5xx) requests**: ES|QL count where `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]`.\n- **Requests and 5xx errors over time**: line chart of request count by time bucket. Despite the title, it plots only total requests, not 5xx. I couldn't get a second series to validate, so I dropped it.\n\n**Rule: `EFF-1e537256 5xx spike`** (id `ce1f4e14-6f57-447b-9542-91158164a142`)\n- ES Query rule, enabled, schedule every 1m.\n- Counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]` over a 5m window on `@timestamp`.\n- Fires when count is `> 10`. No actions attached, so it will record alerts but notify nobody.\n- After one cycle, the execution status is `ok`.\n\n**Data findings**\n- The data view has 40+ fields. The ones that matter are `@timestamp`, `response` (a string, so use `response.keyword` for matching), `bytes`, `clientip`, `geo.dest`, `url`, `agent`, and `machine.os`.\n- I couldn't query the index for response-code values or the time range. Kibana's console and internal search proxies are disabled here, and Elasticsearch isn't reachable on port 9200. So the 5xx filter is written against the expected numeric codes, not checked against the data.\n\n**Gaps to know about**\n- The sample data is from 2018, so the rule will almost certainly not fire on current time. That's expected, but it means I haven't seen it fire.\n- The ES|QL 5xx metric panel's query hasn't been run. The dashboard accepted it, but I couldn't render it to confirm.\n- The first Elasticsearch query I sent hung and was moved to background task `bc5obqj1k`. It's probably still waiting and should time out on its own.",
